<GuideLayout
  title="Anatomical Panel Design for Performance Activewear"
  type="DESIGN ENGINEERING"
  description="How anatomical panel design improves fit, mobility, compression placement and visual shaping in leggings, sports bras and performance activewear."
>
  <p>Anatomical panel design means building activewear around body movement instead of treating a garment as a flat style sketch. For leggings, sports bras and training tops, panel placement affects stretch, support, opacity, seam comfort and how premium the product feels on the body.</p>

  <h2>Why Panel Placement Matters</h2>
  <p>A strong panel plan helps control compression zones, reduce twisting during workouts and create cleaner shaping lines. It also gives product teams more control over fabric direction, seam stress and size grading.</p>

  <h2>Key Areas to Review</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Waistband height and elastic recovery for leggings.</li>
    <li>Gusset shape and crotch seam stress points.</li>
    <li>Side panels for visual shaping and pocket placement.</li>
    <li>Sports bra underband support, neckline coverage and strap angle.</li>
    <li>Mesh or ventilation panels for heat zones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Manufacturing Notes</h2>
  <p>Factories should review panel design during sampling, not only after bulk production. Fitclo checks pattern balance, stretch direction, seam allowance and size grading before confirming a production-ready design.</p>

  <h2>Best Fit for Brands</h2>
  <p>Anatomical panel design is especially useful for premium leggings, high-support sports bras, compression sets and performance-focused gym wear collections where fit is part of the product value.</p>
</GuideLayout>
